WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:01.113
- What's going on everybody?

00:00:01.113 --> 00:00:03.390
my name is Sylvan LaCue
from Miami, Florida.

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:04.420
Wise, wise, wise.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.633
And you are now listening to
the Monday to Monday playlist,

00:00:06.633 --> 00:00:08.730
from my man Gary Vee and my boy.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:09.833
- How are you man?

00:00:09.833 --> 00:00:10.666
- Good to see you.

00:00:10.666 --> 00:00:11.907
- It's good to see you.

00:00:11.907 --> 00:00:12.740
- You well?

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:13.573
- I'm fantastic, alright.

00:00:13.573 --> 00:00:14.406
- Good, man.

00:00:14.406 --> 00:00:15.239
- Absolutely.

00:00:15.239 --> 00:00:16.072
- Yo.

00:00:16.072 --> 00:00:17.025
(mumbling)

00:00:17.025 --> 00:00:17.858
- Jonathan

00:00:17.858 --> 00:00:18.691
- Jonathan, how are you man?

00:00:18.691 --> 00:00:19.524
- Amir.

00:00:19.524 --> 00:00:20.357
- Good to see you, man.

00:00:20.357 --> 00:00:21.193
- Good to see you.

00:00:21.193 --> 00:00:22.026
- How you been?

00:00:22.026 --> 00:00:23.020
- Good, how about yourself?

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:23.853
- I've been well.

00:00:23.853 --> 00:00:25.490
I have been well.

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:26.323
It's ugly out there, huh?

00:00:26.323 --> 00:00:27.156
- Yeah.

00:00:27.156 --> 00:00:28.310
(mumbling)

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:29.470
- It's special outside.

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:31.020
- [Gary] It is special outside.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:35.172
- It's definitely war,
you know what I mean?

00:00:35.172 --> 00:00:36.205
(laughing)

00:00:36.205 --> 00:00:37.951
- [Gary] A different form.

00:00:37.951 --> 00:00:39.278
(laughing)

00:00:39.278 --> 00:00:41.250
- But yeah, so they're in town.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:42.610
He leaves tomorrow.

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:43.660
He just did a sold out show in LA,

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:45.315
sold out show in New York.

00:00:45.315 --> 00:00:46.370
- How was it?

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:47.529
- [Amir] Fantastic.

00:00:47.529 --> 00:00:49.150
- Definitely one of those moments that

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:53.372
had to culminate, rare
culmination to like that place.

00:00:53.372 --> 00:00:56.180
Lot of hard work, just really
grateful, taking it all in.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:57.629
- How long were the shows.

00:00:57.629 --> 00:01:02.190
- I believe New York show's
about an hour and 15 for my set,

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:03.900
so overall two hours.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.740
Same thing for LA as well,
maybe a little bit longer in LA.

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:08.720
Had a little bit more lead time.

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.215
So it was great, man.

00:01:10.215 --> 00:01:11.503
- Good for you, man.

00:01:11.503 --> 00:01:13.620
- I think we jump right into it.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:15.090
But one thing that's so interesting,

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:16.950
the reason why this meeting's
so important is like,

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:21.090
he has this song Empathy,
Gratitude, Selfish,

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:23.340
but I think so in line
with what Gary says about

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:24.990
you gotta cut out your loser friend

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:27.719
or you gotta, you gotta be selfish.

00:01:27.719 --> 00:01:29.214
- To be selfless.

00:01:29.214 --> 00:01:30.047
- You struggle with that.

00:01:30.047 --> 00:01:31.840
You have struggled.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:33.190
So where are you at right now?

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:37.080
- I mean, I still, it's still
something I struggle with.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:39.510
I don't think you ever completely

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:43.040
stop having those moments, right?

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:44.370
Because you know, you're a caring person.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:47.450
So you genuinely are gonna go through it

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:48.310
from time after time.

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:51.090
I think it's just become cognitive
of it and conscious of it

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.920
so that you can see when it's happening.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:56.560
So I've gotten a lot better at it

00:01:57.514 --> 00:01:58.810
just in terms of taking time for myself

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:01.040
and making sure that I can fill my cup.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.280
But I don't think it's
something that ever goes away.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:05.430
You just become conscious
of it and do better at it

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:06.540
step by step by step.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:08.410
- When it's your demeanor, right?

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:12.050
Either you're giving energy
or you're taking energy.

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:13.920
So I've been thinking,
it's actually really funny

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:14.770
we're having this conversation,

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:16.620
I thought about it a lot this morning

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:19.190
when I was working out
on the way into work,

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:23.320
it's like my natural default is to give.

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:25.170
It's why good has happened.

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:28.388
But there's always a breaking point.

00:02:28.388 --> 00:02:29.859
You know?

00:02:29.859 --> 00:02:30.692
- Mm hmm.

00:02:32.737 --> 00:02:34.740
- And there's a couple different
relationships right now

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:36.560
where I'm just like fuck,

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.970
they're about to walk into a buzzsaw,

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:41.820
'cause I haven't given
them any indication,

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:44.120
that's the one thing I
wanna do a better job of.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:45.453
I feel like I,

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.250
I don't wanna just default
into I'm a good person,

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.740
and then just,

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:55.650
one of the things I'm
most embarrassed of ever

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:57.750
is when I was a teenager
and my early twenties,

00:02:57.750 --> 00:02:59.620
the way I would break up with a girl

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:02.228
was so disrespectful and not,

00:03:02.228 --> 00:03:04.960
just genuinely not a man about it.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:07.000
I would just disappear.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.018
It was easy with no technology.

00:03:09.018 --> 00:03:11.268
(laughing)

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:14.450
But, but, but, at the same token,

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:16.636
it would have meant that I would have been

00:03:16.636 --> 00:03:18.610
absolutely that person that just

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:20.080
everything was great for nine months

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:21.740
and then I would just
text and be like I'm out.

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:25.830
So I don't judge people period,

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:26.820
because I think it's a bad idea,

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:29.240
but I think the thing that I'm constantly,

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:30.930
even at 42 I'm like,

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:32.520
I've gotta do a better job,

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:33.860
I'm doing a way better,

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:36.450
I'm not anywhere close to that version,

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:39.230
but more indicators along the way of

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:41.480
this isn't or cool or this isn't right.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:42.360
- Yeah.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:43.510
- So that it's not just

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:47.181
like fuck you this is over.

00:03:47.181 --> 00:03:49.170
What is that?

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:51.660
Pigs get fat, hogs get slaughtered.

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:52.930
Or something.

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:55.086
My natural demeanor is to give so much

00:03:55.086 --> 00:03:57.080
that the other person doesn't even realize

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:59.170
that they're becoming a hog.

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:01.010
- Yeah, I can understand that too because,

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:05.680
especially from the value
that you place on that.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:07.320
It's like when you're giving
and giving and giving,

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:10.560
in your mind it's like,
this is what I'm outputting,

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:11.820
this is what my energy is for.

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:14.310
And rightfully so, you do it
from the bottom of your heart,

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:15.550
so it doesn't matter what happens,

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:17.500
where the relationship stands,

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:18.850
when you're giving something

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:20.177
it's genuinely because you want to.

00:04:20.177 --> 00:04:24.930
And at the same time, when
something or certain situations

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:27.210
are happening, you're
like this isn't cool.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:29.900
And for me I've been one of those guys

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:32.930
where sometimes I just internalize things,

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:35.070
and then when I'm ready, I'm ready.

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:39.071
And I don't realize that
nobody else has been prepared.

00:04:39.071 --> 00:04:41.690
It's kind of like a blindside.

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:44.460
- Yeah, it's an emotional strength

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:49.460
that, you know, your gift is your curse.

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:53.250
Listen I wouldn't trade it for anything.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:56.360
I wouldn't trade it for anything.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:59.233
But I wanna be more conscious of it,

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:05.773
because even from VaynerMedia
as an 850 person company,

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.020
18 months ago I woke up, I'm like,

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:10.373
my company's entitled.

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:12.790
I'm thinking about my kids, I'm like,

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:14.350
they're gonna be entitled.

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:15.930
Emotionally.

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:17.660
It's emotional.

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:18.493
You know?

00:05:20.479 --> 00:05:21.312
And that's it.

00:05:21.312 --> 00:05:24.500
My family breaks if my mom or I

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:25.838
are not in a good mood for a minute.

00:05:25.838 --> 00:05:27.710
One minute.

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:29.972
One minute at a dinner
table of like, not cool,

00:05:29.972 --> 00:05:32.120
and everyone's like.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:33.920
Even when I did Planet of the Apps,

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:35.950
my energy was so high and awesome,

00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:37.240
and there was some stuff going down

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:38.887
that I wasn't pumped with.

00:05:38.887 --> 00:05:39.720
And I was like this next pitch

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:40.590
I'm just gonna shut down,

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:44.050
and the whole, everyone
was like what the fuck.

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:47.020
- How do you deal with
that type of burden?

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:48.640
Knowing that there's--

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:50.866
- I'm grateful, first of
all, 'cause it's power.

00:05:50.866 --> 00:05:54.223
Nobody has leverage over me.

00:05:56.367 --> 00:05:57.880
I prefer it.

00:05:57.880 --> 00:05:59.083
It's the leverage.

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:03.766
So the burden now becomes more of like,

00:06:03.766 --> 00:06:07.940
even in your full right,
don't have a macro,

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:10.490
to me, I'm never gonna
feel bad about blindsiding,

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:11.323
like, you're fired.

00:06:11.323 --> 00:06:12.480
"What the fuck?"

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:15.010
Or like, yo, I don't wanna work
with you like that anymore,

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:18.210
or you know what, you're asking too,

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:19.940
people ask, ask, ask, ask, ask,

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:22.580
and then it's finally
like you know what, nah.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:23.413
Now what?

00:06:23.413 --> 00:06:24.246
What are you gonna do about it?

00:06:24.246 --> 00:06:25.300
'Cause I've got leverage.

00:06:26.428 --> 00:06:29.050
What I think I'm doing with it

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:31.200
is just trying to be more conscious.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:33.123
I don't wanna sandbag people.

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:35.630
There's a fine line between,

00:06:35.630 --> 00:06:37.400
look everybody's a big boy and girl,

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:40.800
and you expect people to,
when you internalize it,

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:42.350
what we're talking about right now,

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:44.030
what you're doing is you're
hoping the other person

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:47.900
recognizes how good it is, and
what value they're getting,

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:50.533
and why do they keep
asking for more inches.

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:55.070
So I don't feel bad for the
person that's taking too much

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:57.420
and hasn't figured out how to do it.

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:00.201
I just wanna be the best
version of myself that I can be.

00:07:00.201 --> 00:07:01.070
(laughing)

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:02.224
Right?

00:07:02.224 --> 00:07:03.569
- Sounds very familiar.

00:07:03.569 --> 00:07:05.750
(laughing)

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:06.660
- So that's it.

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:08.110
That's kinda how I think about it.

00:07:08.110 --> 00:07:09.724
- Got ya.

00:07:09.724 --> 00:07:11.832
(laughing)

00:07:11.832 --> 00:07:14.470
(mumbling)

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:17.240
But I wanted to, how
did you guys meet, man?

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:18.073
How did this start?

00:07:18.073 --> 00:07:20.001
We all know who you are, Amir.

00:07:20.001 --> 00:07:22.622
(mumbling) managers in the game, bro.

00:07:22.622 --> 00:07:23.455
- I appreciate that.

00:07:23.455 --> 00:07:24.700
I'm honestly, I'm grateful
for that for real.

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:29.220
Man, long story, like
the long story short,

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:31.255
we actually met at SXSW, we got introduced

00:07:31.255 --> 00:07:33.370
by a gentleman by the
name of Mike Shahade,

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:35.980
but I don't remember
because unfortunately--

00:07:35.980 --> 00:07:37.990
- [Gary] That's just that world.

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:39.420
- Well it was actually a melee,

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:42.063
ASAP mob was fighting,
and I had (mumbling) DMs.

00:07:42.063 --> 00:07:43.490
(laughing)

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:44.460
God rest is soul.

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:47.850
And so my fatherly leader instinct

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:49.790
was to run to see what's going on,

00:07:49.790 --> 00:07:51.330
but then fast forward a year later,

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:54.300
he was coming to do some
content with Alt-F Digital.

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:57.287
And he killed it, we met,

00:07:57.287 --> 00:07:59.750
probably, it wasn't on the shoot,

00:07:59.750 --> 00:08:01.680
but we met right after the shoot.

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:03.810
And then he just was, I don't know,

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:06.850
he's just considerate, he's
kind, I like the music.

00:08:06.850 --> 00:08:09.270
He wasn't on the Apologies Advanced,

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:11.330
you hadn't worked on any
of those records yet.

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:12.163
We just kept in touch.

00:08:12.163 --> 00:08:14.740
He came back to do another
piece of content with us

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:17.940
and crushed it, crushing it for real.

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:20.478
And I found out he actually came down from

00:08:20.478 --> 00:08:23.390
Oakland to LA on a bus.

00:08:23.390 --> 00:08:25.330
And I was pissed at my
team 'cause I was like

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:27.720
yo, you could have just flown him, right.

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:29.170
But you came in the same day?

00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:31.000
- Yeah, I came in that day, took a bus,

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:33.344
then knew I could be around those artists,

00:08:33.344 --> 00:08:34.901
and then a took bus right back.

00:08:34.901 --> 00:08:35.740
- Yeah, and then a couple months later,

00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:38.360
he was like I wanna sit
down, didn't even ask

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:41.960
for me to manage him, but
we just kinda hit it off

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:44.399
and I saw something
there, and then I had to

00:08:44.399 --> 00:08:46.200
do one more thing on my checklist,

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:48.030
which was to see him perform.

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:49.550
I saw him perform, it was like he put his

00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:51.850
own headliner tour together
called the Loner Tour.

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:53.697
December 2016?

00:08:53.697 --> 00:08:54.530
- Yeah.

00:08:54.530 --> 00:08:55.837
- And then after that
it was like official.

00:08:55.837 --> 00:08:59.322
- It's funny, the bus
story is super interesting.

00:08:59.322 --> 00:09:04.322
I've been thinking a lot
about humility and hunger.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:07.646
I like putting letters together.

00:09:07.646 --> 00:09:09.896
(laughing)

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:13.867
The passion and patience
thing really worked for me.

00:09:13.867 --> 00:09:17.780
That's just real humility and real hunger.

00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:20.650
It really works.

00:09:20.650 --> 00:09:22.579
- He was hungry, I could
see it in his face.

00:09:22.579 --> 00:09:25.080
- People are hungry and fancy.

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:25.913
- Yeah, that's right.

00:09:25.913 --> 00:09:27.341
No, no, that's right.

00:09:27.341 --> 00:09:28.399
He wasn't.

00:09:28.399 --> 00:09:30.870
- You know and people
are humble and passive.

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:34.860
But if you have humility and hunger.

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:36.441
- He definitely had it.

00:09:36.441 --> 00:09:37.727
I mean afterwards I thought about it

00:09:37.727 --> 00:09:39.960
I was like wow this guy's
going back to Oakland,

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:41.630
and he'd just come from Oakland on a bus.

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:45.260
I would have thought his
performance would have been, eh.

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:46.093
- Sure.

00:09:46.093 --> 00:09:47.820
- This is after the fact.

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:49.270
I was like yo, he killed it.

00:09:49.270 --> 00:09:50.103
Who are you?

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:52.686
And that's how I felt.

00:09:52.686 --> 00:09:53.995
I felt something.

00:09:53.995 --> 00:09:55.140
- [Gary] Talent.

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:55.973
- Yeah, talent.

00:09:55.973 --> 00:09:57.160
- [Gary] It's talent.

00:09:57.160 --> 00:09:58.460
When you have real talent,

00:09:59.392 --> 00:10:03.500
if it's parented right, if
the environment's right,

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:05.580
I think real talent comes,

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:07.853
I think humility naturally
comes with talent.

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:09.830
I really do.

00:10:09.830 --> 00:10:11.250
I think are people confused.

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:13.550
I think it's the reverse
of what people think.

00:10:13.550 --> 00:10:16.670
Because if you have talent,

00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:18.860
and you're self aware about it,

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:23.860
you actually are humbled
by it and more importantly,

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:28.920
you feel like whether it's inevitable or,

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:31.061
you're just patient with it.

00:10:31.061 --> 00:10:34.820
I watch people say it's
just a matter of time

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:36.483
and it's fake bravado.

00:10:37.780 --> 00:10:38.613
I see it every day.

00:10:38.613 --> 00:10:39.590
Just a matter of time,

00:10:39.590 --> 00:10:41.351
every day on social especially.

00:10:41.351 --> 00:10:45.193
Just a matter of time
before I, but it's fake.

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:49.410
And then I see the version
of just the matter of time,

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:51.270
and it's taking a bus.

00:10:51.270 --> 00:10:52.763
I'll do anything still.

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:56.190
What I really like about humility

00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:59.163
is it's the core to longevity.

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:05.596
Because you never, I will
never go to that other spot.

00:11:05.596 --> 00:11:08.383
I'm just gonna stay in this lane.

00:11:08.383 --> 00:11:12.550
I hear and see everything
that's happening with me,

00:11:12.550 --> 00:11:15.200
I just almost weirdly don't accept it.

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:16.460
You know?

00:11:16.460 --> 00:11:17.933
I'm unable to,

00:11:19.150 --> 00:11:21.809
and the people closest to
me, like my assistants,

00:11:21.809 --> 00:11:25.460
Babin or Drock, there's always something

00:11:26.486 --> 00:11:30.900
that I do that I can
see through their eyes

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:33.410
them saying, "Why's he doing that?"

00:11:33.410 --> 00:11:35.039
- It's funny you say that.

00:11:35.039 --> 00:11:39.340
Because I've been trying
to pinpoint that emotion

00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:40.800
for a very, very long time.

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:42.470
Probably like the last
six to seven months.

00:11:42.470 --> 00:11:43.764
Because things have been--

00:11:43.764 --> 00:11:44.597
- [Gary] Happening.

00:11:44.597 --> 00:11:46.605
- Happening, you know, repeatedly.

00:11:46.605 --> 00:11:48.600
And I think when we sold out Los Angeles,

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:49.510
which was our first time every

00:11:49.510 --> 00:11:51.020
selling out a show in Los Angeles,

00:11:51.020 --> 00:11:52.448
we've done tons and tons of shows.

00:11:52.448 --> 00:11:53.281
- And how many people?

00:11:53.281 --> 00:11:54.114
- It was 300.

00:11:54.114 --> 00:11:54.947
- Go ahead.

00:11:55.959 --> 00:11:56.792
- I've been in that
position where I'm opening

00:11:56.792 --> 00:11:58.890
for artists at that level
on a consistent basis.

00:11:58.890 --> 00:12:01.193
So when that was happening
and everybody's celebrating,

00:12:01.193 --> 00:12:04.650
obviously I'm taking this moment in,

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:07.777
but there's always that part
of me that's just like...

00:12:09.027 --> 00:12:12.650
- There's a big delta between
300 and Madison Square Garden.

00:12:12.650 --> 00:12:14.280
- Yeah.

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:15.153
- For me too.

00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:17.483
I get it, man.

00:12:18.455 --> 00:12:20.423
- But it's also this very,

00:12:21.358 --> 00:12:24.280
you feel so grateful
for something happening,

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:28.510
that it's almost like you don't
want it to get to your head.

00:12:28.510 --> 00:12:30.400
- It's humility and hunger, man.

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:33.300
You got the humility to be
like, "Wow, this is amazing,"

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:34.817
but you're hungry and thinking,

00:12:34.817 --> 00:12:36.317
"You know what's better than 300?

00:12:36.317 --> 00:12:38.020
"30,000."

00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:40.750
To me, and listen man,

00:12:40.750 --> 00:12:42.580
it's gonna get more and more intense.

00:12:42.580 --> 00:12:44.610
Where I'm sitting right now in my life,

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:48.010
culturally, financially,
impact, it's very high.

00:12:48.010 --> 00:12:49.800
I know where I'm at.

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:52.350
And man, I don't feel
any different than I did

00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:57.320
the first day I did a
flea market when I was 14.

00:12:57.320 --> 00:12:59.140
It's crazy.

00:12:59.140 --> 00:12:59.990
It's crazy.

00:12:59.990 --> 00:13:04.250
It's almost like something
is keeping me in a bubble

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:09.250
outside of all the admiration and success.

00:13:09.310 --> 00:13:10.950
I don't know what else to tell you.

00:13:10.950 --> 00:13:12.233
It's humility and hunger.

00:13:13.114 --> 00:13:15.896
I'm humbled by it, I
don't take it for granted.

00:13:15.896 --> 00:13:18.830
I don't think I'm special.

00:13:18.830 --> 00:13:20.360
I think it's part of the output.

00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:21.540
I think my parents are special,

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:24.053
I think my circumstance was special.

00:13:25.950 --> 00:13:27.633
And I'm just still so hungry.

00:13:30.490 --> 00:13:31.953
You know?

00:13:31.953 --> 00:13:33.179
- Yeah, stay hungry.

00:13:33.179 --> 00:13:34.012
- That's wild.

00:13:34.012 --> 00:13:36.870
- And I think if that's foundational,

00:13:36.870 --> 00:13:40.970
I think I don't know who they are,

00:13:40.970 --> 00:13:45.100
but somebody won four
Grammys and did a show

00:13:45.100 --> 00:13:48.660
two days earlier to 81,000, and felt

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:51.260
the same way I feel right
now or you feel right now.

00:13:53.210 --> 00:13:54.780
You know?

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:55.750
I don't know.

00:13:55.750 --> 00:13:57.240
- That's a good point to make,
come to that type of emotion.

00:13:57.240 --> 00:13:59.153
- Yeah, if it's there, it's there.

00:14:01.631 --> 00:14:02.670
I don't know.

00:14:02.670 --> 00:14:05.220
There's nothing I've seen,
I've never been like,

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:10.220
you made it, or I'm always
like humility, I'm like cool.

00:14:10.470 --> 00:14:13.520
Nightline or a cover of a magazine or

00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:15.830
somebody cool DMs me.

00:14:15.830 --> 00:14:18.493
I'm like yeah, I'm like this is cool.

00:14:18.493 --> 00:14:20.930
- [Amir] This is dope, but
still, there's something else.

00:14:20.930 --> 00:14:23.430
- There's never been anything like--

00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:25.053
- I also think what's great about that

00:14:25.053 --> 00:14:27.940
is because it's also rooted in love.

00:14:27.940 --> 00:14:30.520
So love doesn't really have a price.

00:14:30.520 --> 00:14:31.957
So it doesn't matter what is you do.

00:14:31.957 --> 00:14:36.140
You can attain 20,000 buildings and

00:14:36.140 --> 00:14:38.760
sell out as many concerts
and win 85 Grammys

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:40.710
and be respected by your peers,

00:14:40.710 --> 00:14:42.290
and do all the coolest
things in the world,

00:14:42.290 --> 00:14:45.990
but the love of what you're
doing is immeasurable.

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:47.433
There's no price tag to it.

00:14:49.142 --> 00:14:50.080
- [Gary] And it's also unlimited.

00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:52.110
- Yes, definitely.

00:14:52.110 --> 00:14:53.960
- So I think that's the thing for me.

00:14:54.940 --> 00:14:55.830
It's unlimited.

00:14:55.830 --> 00:14:58.690
There is, it's not even,

00:14:58.690 --> 00:15:00.470
to your point of measurability,

00:15:00.470 --> 00:15:01.750
there is no finish line.

00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:03.720
I mean, for me it's impact.

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:05.280
There is no, like what?

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:07.663
I'm not to gonna get
to 7.8 billion people.

00:15:10.390 --> 00:15:12.100
The pope doesn't do that.

00:15:12.100 --> 00:15:13.520
Jesus Christ doesn't do that.

00:15:13.520 --> 00:15:14.650
Mohammad Ali didn't do that.

00:15:14.650 --> 00:15:15.490
Gandhi didn't do that.

00:15:15.490 --> 00:15:17.000
Martin Luther King didn't.

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:18.530
You're not gonna get to everybody.

00:15:18.530 --> 00:15:21.796
So to me it's almost
like, well let me try.

00:15:21.796 --> 00:15:24.046
(laughing)

00:15:26.550 --> 00:15:31.393
Meaning, and again, it's
about self awareness.

00:15:31.393 --> 00:15:35.660
Right now I'm laughing,
'cause I know that's on film

00:15:35.660 --> 00:15:37.207
and people are gonna
watch that and be like,

00:15:37.207 --> 00:15:41.085
"This guy's fucking,
the ego is unbearable."

00:15:41.085 --> 00:15:43.400
I think people are confused.

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:45.343
It's the reverse.

00:15:45.343 --> 00:15:49.203
If you live your life in a way like that,

00:15:50.090 --> 00:15:52.280
it's not that I expect
to be on that pedestal,

00:15:52.280 --> 00:15:55.280
or to be very frank, or even want that.

00:15:55.280 --> 00:15:58.203
It's why not bring that much good?

00:15:59.569 --> 00:16:01.730
- [Sylvan] Yeah, that's right.

00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:04.453
- To me, why not?

00:16:05.430 --> 00:16:08.883
What's the collateral of that ambition?

00:16:09.800 --> 00:16:11.270
'Cause that's my life.

00:16:11.270 --> 00:16:14.140
My life is gonna be the
collateral of the ambition

00:16:14.140 --> 00:16:16.993
to make that kind of impact
and have that kind of legacy.

00:16:19.240 --> 00:16:20.690
That's what my day to day is.

00:16:21.970 --> 00:16:24.180
And what's really interesting for me

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:26.800
is unlike you and others,

00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:31.214
I didn't grow up doing
something that made me think,

00:16:31.214 --> 00:16:33.650
or aspiring to do something
that made me think

00:16:33.650 --> 00:16:35.300
I could have that kind of impact.

00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:39.220
That's the great misunderstanding
of this amazing time,

00:16:39.220 --> 00:16:44.220
is I, pre-internet, would
have been a good business man,

00:16:44.760 --> 00:16:48.869
affected the 1,000 to 5,000
people that worked with me,

00:16:48.869 --> 00:16:53.869
and probably just 87 to 92 that
worked close enough with me.

00:16:55.190 --> 00:16:58.003
My family, my kids, and
I woulda called it a day.

00:16:58.930 --> 00:17:02.540
Because I didn't do music or politics or

00:17:03.460 --> 00:17:05.420
write novels or poetry.

00:17:05.420 --> 00:17:06.823
I wasn't doing art.

00:17:08.710 --> 00:17:10.593
Which is how you actually get there.

00:17:12.220 --> 00:17:14.190
But I feel like how I build businesses

00:17:14.190 --> 00:17:16.050
and things of that nature is that.

00:17:16.050 --> 00:17:19.120
It just, we didn't live
in a world where that was,

00:17:19.120 --> 00:17:20.700
as a matter of fact, I've
been spending a lot of time

00:17:20.700 --> 00:17:23.143
looking at entrepreneurs in the 1800s.

00:17:24.780 --> 00:17:27.380
They made so much of the
good impact in our society.

00:17:28.590 --> 00:17:32.231
In the days of kings and early
countries being established,

00:17:32.231 --> 00:17:35.840
it's funny, I've always
not liked the narrative

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:38.500
of bussinesses have a responsibility.

00:17:38.500 --> 00:17:43.500
Meaning not how the Republicans
or Democrats looked at it,

00:17:43.510 --> 00:17:46.280
some of it was too bullshit
and just for the money,

00:17:46.280 --> 00:17:49.730
and others were too
altruistic and not practical.

00:17:49.730 --> 00:17:52.080
But I have been trying
to find that middle zone

00:17:53.006 --> 00:17:56.180
of what's happening with me and now.

00:17:56.180 --> 00:17:57.580
Spending a lot of time on it.

00:17:57.580 --> 00:18:02.430
But it's really cool to
think about this conversation

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:07.280
in a macro, meaning something's
clearly happening where

00:18:08.280 --> 00:18:10.140
the level of conversation is just

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:12.390
going in the right direction.

00:18:12.390 --> 00:18:14.405
- I think it has too overall.

00:18:14.405 --> 00:18:19.270
Coming down to, especially
coming down to how we're

00:18:19.270 --> 00:18:20.562
internally feeling, and that's what I

00:18:20.562 --> 00:18:23.400
tried to encompass with my last album,

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:24.830
Apologies in Advance.

00:18:24.830 --> 00:18:26.466
What we try to encompass with our label

00:18:26.466 --> 00:18:28.660
and our company, Wise Up.

00:18:28.660 --> 00:18:31.070
It's really sparking the conversation

00:18:31.070 --> 00:18:34.150
so that we can change and
also alter perspective.

00:18:34.150 --> 00:18:37.980
- You should put out more
content that isn't music.

00:18:37.980 --> 00:18:39.210
- [Sylvan] Yeah, I agree.

00:18:39.210 --> 00:18:41.948
I think that is the
gateway to your unlock.

00:18:41.948 --> 00:18:45.940
If I had music talent
and put out an album,

00:18:45.940 --> 00:18:47.123
it would do real well.

00:18:48.450 --> 00:18:50.223
- [Amir] You've got a nice audience.

00:18:50.223 --> 00:18:55.223
- I think not knowing
how you create music,

00:18:55.420 --> 00:18:57.830
a lot of times, you guys know this I know,

00:18:57.830 --> 00:18:59.530
I'm always like more music, more music,

00:18:59.530 --> 00:19:01.760
mainly 'cause that's one's outlet.

00:19:01.760 --> 00:19:04.670
I think just the general
thoughtfulness of your demeanor,

00:19:04.670 --> 00:19:07.210
I think you should do more of what I do.

00:19:07.210 --> 00:19:11.910
I think you should do a
30 minute call in show

00:19:11.910 --> 00:19:14.810
where people just call in and
ask you questions like I do.

00:19:15.886 --> 00:19:17.412
- [Sylvan] Wow.

00:19:17.412 --> 00:19:19.177
- Yeah, I just think
that you're interested

00:19:19.177 --> 00:19:23.560
and vibed towards other things
that I think are important,

00:19:23.560 --> 00:19:28.100
in the same way that if you
look at the genre of hip hop,

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:31.370
people are just hip hop
artists, and then they became

00:19:31.370 --> 00:19:35.409
culturally relevant individuals,
business men and women.

00:19:35.409 --> 00:19:40.409
I just think that you have white space to

00:19:42.020 --> 00:19:44.470
have a different conversation
with your audience.

00:19:46.190 --> 00:19:47.529
- [Amir] Agreed.

00:19:47.529 --> 00:19:48.663
So now you've got some homework.

00:19:49.551 --> 00:19:50.407
- [Sylvan] Pretty much so.

00:19:50.407 --> 00:19:51.630
- You know?

00:19:51.630 --> 00:19:52.680
I just think it would be interesting

00:19:52.680 --> 00:19:53.930
to see if you went there.

00:19:55.766 --> 00:19:56.599
- I think he should.

00:19:56.599 --> 00:19:58.887
I mean he had put an email address

00:19:58.887 --> 00:19:59.810
in his profile on Twitter,

00:19:59.810 --> 00:20:01.637
and he's like, "Hey, if you're
going through some shit,

00:20:01.637 --> 00:20:03.040
"send me an email."

00:20:03.040 --> 00:20:05.000
And he legit was going
through all those emails.

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:07.070
So I mean, but being able to do that.

00:20:07.070 --> 00:20:08.751
I think it's sincere.

00:20:08.751 --> 00:20:12.230
- I used to do that, 2007,
2011, that's all I did.

00:20:12.230 --> 00:20:14.310
And then I've made it more outwardly

00:20:14.310 --> 00:20:15.800
because what I realized is cool

00:20:15.800 --> 00:20:17.340
I can answer that kid's question

00:20:17.340 --> 00:20:19.230
or that grown up's question,

00:20:19.230 --> 00:20:20.690
but it was a one to one thing,

00:20:20.690 --> 00:20:24.100
meanwhile 30% of my audience
was dealing with the same shit.

00:20:24.100 --> 00:20:25.847
- I like the call in idea a lot, though.

00:20:25.847 --> 00:20:27.145
I think that's dope.

00:20:27.145 --> 00:20:28.220
And I think it will be impactful

00:20:28.220 --> 00:20:30.132
because he's sincere and genuine.

00:20:30.132 --> 00:20:31.283
- And I also think it's good
content, it's good content.

00:20:32.338 --> 00:20:33.270
You put that on YouTube,
you put it in a podcast,

00:20:33.270 --> 00:20:35.120
you put it on Facebook and Instagram,

00:20:36.724 --> 00:20:39.070
and you know, you're just
building an authentic,

00:20:39.070 --> 00:20:41.440
all it comes down to is relationships.

00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:44.043
And there's a lot of ways
to have relationships.

00:20:44.043 --> 00:20:48.890
I just really, I feel
like I'm going through

00:20:50.260 --> 00:20:53.755
with my business content
and personal content

00:20:53.755 --> 00:20:56.530
something very similar to
putting out a hit album

00:20:56.530 --> 00:20:57.947
that reached people.

00:21:00.772 --> 00:21:03.163
- [Sylvan] That's very true.

00:21:03.163 --> 00:21:05.400
- And those 300 people
that came to the show,

00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:07.010
it wasn't a normal 300.

00:21:07.010 --> 00:21:08.927
The videos I've seen, the crowd was going,

00:21:08.927 --> 00:21:11.750
you go so deep with your fans,

00:21:11.750 --> 00:21:13.240
'cause you're putting your heart into it.

00:21:13.240 --> 00:21:16.030
You're going so much deeper
than a lot of artists.

00:21:16.030 --> 00:21:17.990
- So what's happening now?

00:21:17.990 --> 00:21:20.660
- Right now, we're post
Apologies in Advance,

00:21:20.660 --> 00:21:24.248
so we've been releasing records,
working on another record

00:21:24.248 --> 00:21:27.420
that's slated for some
time later this year.

00:21:27.420 --> 00:21:30.960
Don't want to spill on that aspect.

00:21:30.960 --> 00:21:32.349
Possibly planning another tour.

00:21:32.349 --> 00:21:33.591
- Do you write?

00:21:33.591 --> 00:21:34.919
- I write everything.

00:21:34.919 --> 00:21:36.260
- And what is, do you just like,

00:21:36.260 --> 00:21:38.080
if it randomly hits you
you just start writing

00:21:38.080 --> 00:21:39.740
or you have a set regiment or?

00:21:39.740 --> 00:21:42.290
- What's funny is I
usually write all the time.

00:21:42.290 --> 00:21:43.123
- [Gary] You're always writing.

00:21:43.123 --> 00:21:44.440
- Yeah, I'm always writing.

00:21:44.440 --> 00:21:46.040
Usually when I'm creating records

00:21:46.040 --> 00:21:47.510
I usually create the record first.

00:21:47.510 --> 00:21:48.990
So I'll start by producing.

00:21:48.990 --> 00:21:50.210
I also produce all of my content.

00:21:50.210 --> 00:21:53.300
So my last album was
entirely produced by myself.

00:21:53.300 --> 00:21:54.550
And I'll just start writing,

00:21:54.550 --> 00:21:55.910
and then we'll create the actual message

00:21:55.910 --> 00:21:57.710
and formula around it as we're getting

00:21:57.710 --> 00:21:59.220
halfway into the album.

00:21:59.220 --> 00:22:01.330
Every album I do, everybody work I have

00:22:01.330 --> 00:22:04.530
has to come with something
you can do take away and use.

00:22:04.530 --> 00:22:05.370
It has to.

00:22:05.370 --> 00:22:06.560
That's my brand.

00:22:06.560 --> 00:22:07.840
That's what Wise Up stands for.

00:22:07.840 --> 00:22:10.670
It's changing perspectives
and opening conversation.

00:22:10.670 --> 00:22:12.530
So that comes first.

00:22:12.530 --> 00:22:14.100
And then the music comes.

00:22:14.100 --> 00:22:15.706
The music is just the platform for--

00:22:15.706 --> 00:22:16.663
- I really, really think
you would do Q&amp;A, man.

00:22:17.560 --> 00:22:20.750
My point of view, I took
a very different turn

00:22:20.750 --> 00:22:24.113
once I really made it
about them even more.

00:22:25.930 --> 00:22:27.980
I got all sorts of things
going through my head,

00:22:27.980 --> 00:22:30.910
and I'm real quiet with myself
and I have unlimited content,

00:22:30.910 --> 00:22:35.650
but when you're answering questions,

00:22:35.650 --> 00:22:37.720
when you're answering three
or four questions a day,

00:22:37.720 --> 00:22:40.333
five, seven, nine, three, 16,

00:22:41.581 --> 00:22:44.390
you're in listening mode,

00:22:44.390 --> 00:22:46.500
and you have a different level,

00:22:46.500 --> 00:22:50.700
I have such a different level
of pulse than everybody,

00:22:50.700 --> 00:22:52.650
because I'm listening.

00:22:52.650 --> 00:22:56.250
People just see the output of me talking.

00:22:56.250 --> 00:22:58.986
They don't see my 24 hours.

00:22:58.986 --> 00:23:00.180
Right?

00:23:00.180 --> 00:23:01.170
They see me interviewing somebody.

00:23:01.170 --> 00:23:02.670
Gary you're always talking,
you're always talking.

00:23:02.670 --> 00:23:06.100
